# Redirects for Cloudflare Pages — the replacement for the `return 301` locations in
# nginx/indianinprague.conf. Copied into dist/ by scripts/build-dist.mjs; Pages reads it
# from the deployment root and does not serve it.
#
# Format: <from> <to> <status>. Limits: 2,000 static + 100 dynamic rules.
#
# The homepage. `html_handling: "none"` in wrangler.jsonc makes the asset server serve the
# literal path requested and nothing else — which is exactly what keeps /wardrobe.html
# answering 200 instead of redirecting to /wardrobe, and is the whole reason this site is on
# Workers Static Assets rather than Pages. The cost of that setting is that "/" no longer
# maps to index.html by itself: measured against a local dev server, the bare homepage
# returned 404 and served 404.html.
#
# The `200` status makes this a REWRITE, not a redirect: the visitor stays on "/" and the
# bytes of index.html are returned there. A 301 to /index.html would work too and would be
# wrong — it would put a redirect on the most-linked URL of the site and split it from the
# canonical tag, which is the same trap that ruled Pages out.
/            /index.html      200

# NOT here: /404.html is served automatically for unmatched paths, via
# not_found_handling in wrangler.jsonc.
#
# Also NOT here, and a deliberate change from nginx: the extensionless form of each page
# (/clothes for clothes.html) no longer resolves. nginx offered it via `try_files $uri
# $uri.html`, but nothing in the markup, the sitemap or any canonical tag has ever used it —
# it existed only for URLs typed by hand. Restoring it would mean serving every page at two
# live URLs, which is the duplicate-content problem the .html decision above avoids. The one
# extensionless path that was ever published, /shop, is redirected below.

# shop.html became wardrobe.html when the top nav went to five modules and the word "shop"
# left the visitor-facing site. It was the most linked and best indexed page here, so the
# old path stays alive permanently. 301 and not 302: the move is permanent, and only a 301
# passes the accumulated ranking to the new URL. Both forms, because both resolved before.
/shop.html   /wardrobe.html   301
/shop        /wardrobe.html   301
